#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""claude-docker - Run Claude Code inside a sandboxed Docker container.

Builds the sandbox image on first use, then runs claude inside a container
with the project directory mounted read-write and ~/.claude persisted.

  claude-docker [--add-dir DIR]... [--add-dir-ro DIR]... [claude args]...
  claude-docker --shell [bash args]   bash in the same sandbox, not claude
  claude-docker --rebuild        force a clean image rebuild, then exit
  claude-docker --dockerfile     print the generated Dockerfile, then exit
  claude-docker --net-log        domains the egress filter denied, then exit
  claude-docker --net-reload     restart the egress filter, then exit
  claude-docker --trace [list | SESSION] [--follow] [--verbose | --json]
                [--only KIND,...] [--failed]
                                 what the latest session of this project
                                 did, or the one whose id starts with
                                 SESSION, list lists them, --json gives
                                 JSON Lines, --follow shows what the
                                 sessions do as they do it, --only shows
                                 the kinds of event named (bash, net),
                                 --failed what went wrong
  claude-docker --worktree [NAME]   second session on its own checkout

--worktree is claude's own flag, forwarded like any other claude arg: it
creates a git worktree and branch under .claude/worktrees/ in the project,
which the project mount already covers, so parallel sessions do not share
a working tree or index.

Extra project directories (e.g. the frontend repo next to this backend)
are mounted read-write at their host path, get their .env files shadowed
like the main project's, and are forwarded to claude via --add-dir. Also
accepted, colon-separated: CLAUDE_DOCKER_EXTRA_DIRS=~/code/frontend
--add-dir-ro (or CLAUDE_DOCKER_EXTRA_DIRS_RO) does the same read-only, for
reference code the session should read but never change.

The image is assembled from docker: base.Dockerfile, the
selected features/*.Dockerfile, then tail.Dockerfile.

Environment, all optional:
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_FEATURES    features to build in, space or comma separated
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_NET_FILTER  1 restricts egress to allowed-domains.txt
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_NET_LOG     0 stops recording what a session connects to
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_PROC_LOG    0 stops recording the processes of a session
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_TELEMETRY   1 lets Claude Code send usage and crash reports
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_SSH_AGENT   1 forwards the SSH agent
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_SOCKET      1 allows docker ps and logs, full adds inspect
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_RW_CONFIG   1 leaves the protected ~/.claude config writable
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_SHARED_HISTORY  1 lets a session see what Claude Code
                            remembers of other projects
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_SHADOW      extra file name globs to hide, colon separated
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_MEMORY      memory limit
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_PIDS        process limit
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_CACHE       0 keeps ~/.cache on a tmpfs, nothing persists
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_RUNTIME     container runtime, e.g. runsc for gVisor
  CLAUDE_DOCKER_SHARED_DIR  scratch directory shared between sessions,
                            empty for none

To override a default for every session, put NAME=value lines in
~/.config/claude-sandbox/config, the environment still wins.

The code is the claude_sandbox package next to this directory: settings.py
(the settings and their defaults), docker.py, image.py, session.py,
network.py, history.py, tamper.py, launcher.py and cli.py for a session,
sidecar.py, capture.py, pcap.py, reader.py, processes.py, procwatch.py,
doors.py, recorder.py, records.py and background.py for the records of
what it did, events.py, transcript.py, shown.py, trace.py, follow.py and
digest.py for --trace and the lines said when a session ends. Standard
library only, and no syntax newer than Python 3.9, the version macOS
ships.
"""

import os
import sys


def main() -> int:
    # The package sits next to bin/ in the checkout, it is not installed.
    # realpath, so this script can be symlinked into a directory on PATH.
    sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))))
    from claude_sandbox.cli import cli

    return cli()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
